--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Development material that ships with the plugin, on purpose
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

One directory in this repository is development material rather than game
code, and a reviewer is right to ask why an installed plugin carries it.

.claude/skills/gauntlet-loop/   (about 8 KB)
    docs/plan.md lists it as a prerequisite: "The gauntlet-loop skill is
    copied into the repository at .claude/skills/gauntlet-loop/." It describes
    the development workflow this repository is built with. The Omarchy shell
    never loads it, it is not referenced by manifest.json or by any QML file,
    and it is attributed above. It is 8 KB of text on the installing machine
    and it changes nothing about what the plugin does.

The multiplayer design mock that used to ship under docs/ (1.78 MB) was
deleted on 2026-09-07, before submission: it pictured a lobby this plugin
does not have, and the solo garage it was a reference for is built.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Game assets
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

assets/karts/<body>/<paint>.png, assets/karts/<body>/meta.json,
assets/karts/manifest.json
    The car sprite sheets: six bodies (coupe, hatch, wedge, saloon, buggy,
    pickup) in eight paints, two cameras, eight yaws and three scales each.
    Original work, generated by this repository's own scripts and nothing
    else: src/tools/bake-cars.py builds each body from primitives in Blender
    (run headless, `blender -b`; no .blend file exists) and src/tools/pxart.py
    reduces the renders to the pixel grid and palette. `npm run sprites`
    regenerates every file byte for byte and `npm run check:sprites` verifies
    the committed files against manifest.json's SHA-256 records. No third-party
    model, texture, font or photograph is involved; the reference image the
    style was judged against, docs/golden-hour-car.png, is not sampled or
    traced. Licence: MIT, with the rest of the repository.

assets/props/*.png, assets/props/props-meta.json, assets/props/manifest.json
    The roadside and effect sprites: gantry, tyre wall, banner, hay bale,
    cone, drum, crowd, boards, water tower, rocks, jetty, pines, bridge,
    roller door, overpass, scrapyard, pile-up, wrench, oil slick, pothole,
    tow hook and hubcaps. Original work, generated by src/tools/bake-props.py
    in Blender (headless, no .blend file) and reduced by src/tools/px-props.py;
    `npm run props` regenerates them byte for byte and `npm run check:props`
    verifies the committed files against manifest.json. Licence: MIT.

assets/sfx/*.wav, assets/sfx/manifest.json
    Fifteen short PCM sound cues, one per power-up event. Synthesised by
    src/tools/bake-sfx.py from the Python standard library alone: nothing is
    downloaded, sampled or recorded, and `npm run check:sfx` holds the
    committed files to the manifest's SHA-256 records. Nobody who worked on
    this repository has listened to them; they are a first pass that waits on
    an ear. Licence: MIT.

shaders/road.frag, shaders/road.frag.qsb
    The road and terrain fragment shader, written for this plugin, and its
    precompiled Qt shader container baked from that source with Qt's `qsb`.
    Licence: MIT.

assets/cards/
    Reserved and empty but for its .gitkeep: the power-up cards are drawn in
    QML. The project's rule for accepting any asset is: original work, or
    CC0, or a licence at least as permissive as MIT, with attribution recorded
    here whether or not the licence demands it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
preview.png
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`preview.png` is the garage as this plugin draws it, captured through the repository's own headless harness (`dev/Harness.qml --screen Garage --shot`) at 1920 by 1080 with the frame-rate overlay off and no control focused.
It is a rendering of the plugin's own screen, not a photograph of a child's machine, and it is retaken whenever the garage changes.

Those two sentences are fixed strings in src/tools/check-readme.ts, and
README.md carries them verbatim too; `npm run check:readme` fails if either
document drops them, or if the file is missing, is not a PNG, or is smaller
than 1280 by 720. To retake it:

    qml -platform offscreen -I dev/imports dev/Harness.qml -- --screen Garage \
        --width 1920 --height 1080 --hud off --focus -1 --shot preview.png --exit

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
How strongly the README's claims are evidenced
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two kinds of claim in README.md rest on different evidence, and a reviewer
should not have to guess which is which.

Executed and reproducible: everything npm run check covers -- the engine
rules and their vectors, the type check, the boundary, hygiene and manifest
rules, the committed bundle's freshness, the README gate itself, and the
marketplace security baseline. Each of those runs on this machine and in CI
on every push.

Read from source rather than executed: the Omarchy 4 command surface named in
README.md -- omarchy plugin add/remove/disable, the omarchy plugin rm alias,
the --yes flag, the o.bind() form for ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua, and the
one-entry-per-disable behaviour of ~/.config/omarchy/shell.json. These were
read from Omarchy's own plugin scripts and from --help output on a
development VM, and one full add-then-remove cycle was executed there on
2026-09-07 against a throwaway clone of this repository under a different
plugin id, with the outcome README.md's Remove section records. No check in
this repository verifies any of it.
